<%#
  The access token travels in a data attribute rather than interpolated into the script body.

  Interpolating it was not exploitable: h() turns " into &quot; and < into &lt;, and HTML
  entities are not decoded inside a script element, so there was no way to produce the quote
  character needed to close the string literal.  But that safety was incidental -- it rested on
  h() happening to cover the one character that mattered, in a context h() is not written for.

  What it did break: h() does not escape backslashes, carriage returns or newlines, so a token
  containing any of those made this script fail to parse.  The token was then never handed over
  and the popup never closed, with nothing in the UI to say why.

  An attribute has a single well-defined escaping rule that the template engine applies for us,
  and getAttribute hands the value to JS without a second escaping context to get wrong.
-%>
<div id="oauth_lander" style="display:none;"
     data-access-token="<%= h(params[:access_token]) %>"></div>

<% javascript_tag do %>
  (function() {
    var el    = document.getElementById("oauth_lander");
    var token = el ? el.getAttribute("data-access-token") : null;

    // The opener is the API Explorer page, same origin as this one.  If this window was opened
    // by a page on another origin, reading window.opener.updateAccessToken throws instead of
    // handing the token over -- which is the behaviour we want, so it is caught and ignored
    // rather than prevented.
    try {
      if (token && window.opener && window.opener.updateAccessToken) {
        window.opener.updateAccessToken(token);
      }
    } catch (e) {
      // same-origin policy refused the handoff; nothing to do but close
    }

    window.close();
  })();
<% end %>
